ing it Lean D. Paxton's financial disadvantage and outside-spending attacks justify a modest Democratic edge, but Texas's Republican lean keeps this short of Likely D.", "watch": "Turnout composition in the Dallas-Fort Worth and suburban counties during early voting could shift the margin either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: John Cornyn (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Texas's open Senate seat features Democrat James Talarico against Republican Ken Paxton after John Cornyn lost renomination. Polls through late September show Talarico leading by one to five points, with recent surveys from Big Data, Marist, and Texas Southern all putting him ahead. Prediction markets give him roughly a 60% chance, and several expert raters call the race Lean D while others call it a Tossup.\n\nMoney is lopsided: Talarico has raised nearly $69 million and holds a large cash advantage, while Paxton faces heavy outside opposition spending. Still, Texas's Republican-leaning electorate and Paxton's incumbent-adjacent name recognition keep the race competitive. The forecast gives Talarico a modest edge, with meaningful uncertainty given the state's partisan lean and a tightening trend in the final stretch.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6697, "margin": 2.08}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6924, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7255, "margin": 2.8}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6997, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6523, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6444, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6983, "margin": 2.5}]}